[#:seon.task{:name "land-is-one-call"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Landing a green proposal took root four hand calls (`my.program/prepare-merge!`, `my.program/accept-merge!`, `my.program/write-back!`, `bin/lane commit`). Done when `seon.cluster.source/land!` takes the proposal and the checkout root and runs `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!`, `seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` (the gate unchanged; `seon.task/decide!` `:accepted`), `seon.cluster.source/write-back!` and `seon.cluster.source/commit!`, answering `:seon.source/landed`, or the first refusal as data with the merge task undecided and nothing written."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/land! seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! seon.cluster.source/accept-merge! seon.cluster.source/write-back! seon.task/decide! my.program/accept-merge!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-function-and-its-schema-land-as-one-unit"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A branch that changed a function and the schema its contract names could not merge: `seon.program/changed-identities` admitted only functions, tests and namespaces, so `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!` refused agent r3-ref-section's `:seon.ref/section-error` fix. Done when `seon.program/changed-identities` admits `:seon.schema/key`, `seon.test/select` seeds a changed schema with the functions whose contracts name it (today it refuses `:seon.test/coverage-unknown`, so the agent's test step and the merge gate refuse), and `seon.cluster.source/write-back!` writes each changed schema into its namespace's resource with rewrite-clj (replaced, added in sorted or append position, removed), proven by reading the resource back to the merged entity's digest (`seon.schema/canonical-schema-entities`), so `seon.cluster.source/land!` lands a producer, its new schema and its consumer in one commit."
             :must-read #{seon.program/changed-identities seon.test/select seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! seon.cluster.source/write-back! seon.cluster.source/land! seon.schema/canonical-schema-entities}}
 #:seon.task{:name "accept-fences-the-target-program-it-tested"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` fenced only the identities the merge changes (`seon.cluster.source/declarations-held-tx`), so a callee replaced on the target between prepare and accept landed a composition no run tested (astra day audit, finding 1). Done when `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!` records the target's basis-t it tested on as `:seon.source/target-basis-t` and the accept transaction refuses, inside the writer, when any program declaration's definition digest changed on the target since then, naming those declarations; non-program facts never stale it; prepare again."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! seon.cluster.source/accept-merge! my.program/prepare-merge!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-10/astra-day-audit.md#target-changes-after-prepare"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-landing-commits-exactly-its-proven-bytes"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/commit!` committed whatever its paths held at commit time, ignoring each file's `:seon.source/candidate-digest`, and never checked the commit's parent or tree (astra day audit, finding 2a). Done when it refuses, committing nothing, a path whose bytes are not the proven candidate, and verifies the commit it made has exactly the previous head as its one parent and holds exactly the candidate bytes for each written path."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/commit! seon.cluster.source/write-back!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-10/astra-day-audit.md#files-and-git"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "one-owner-holds-the-checkout-from-write-back-through-git"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/write-back!` proved and installed a landing's files outside `.git/lane-commit.lock`, which `seon.cluster.source/commit!` took only afterwards, so two landings (or a landing and `bin/lane commit`) could read the same old bytes and replace each other's declarations (astra day audit, finding 2b). Done when `seon.cluster.source/write-and-commit!` holds that lock from the file proof through the verified git commit, and `seon.cluster.source/land!` writes and commits only through it."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/write-back! seon.cluster.source/commit! seon.cluster.source/land!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-10/astra-day-audit.md#files-and-git"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-landing-is-one-git-commit"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A landing's files reached git only through `bin/lane commit`, a Claude-lane tool. Done when `seon.cluster.source/commit!` makes one `git commit --only` of exactly the landing's written paths through `babashka.process/shell`, its message naming the task, merge task, agent, gate run and merge commit, and refuses `:seon.git/commit-error` when anything else is staged in the index (the rule `bin/lane commit` enforces)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/commit! seon.cluster.source/land! babashka.process/shell}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-red-reaching-test-refuses-acceptance"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` gated only the proofs (and each replaced function reaching a green member), so a merge whose proofs were green committed while another test of its run was red, shown only as `:seon.source/red` (AGENTS.md, merge: `merge!` commits only when every test on the merge branch is green; a test already red on main stops it for root). Done when acceptance reads the run's recorded members and refuses `:seon.source/red-test-error` naming each member of the run that is not green (`:seon.source/red-tests`, each with its `:seon.test/sym`, the `:seon.test.run/id` that recorded it and its verdict, red or unknown), and refuses `:seon.source/red-on-target-error` instead when every such test is recorded red on the target for its current reach there; main's head is unchanged."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/accept-merge! seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! seon.test/recorded-result seon.test.runner/reach-digests seon.test/green-members}}
 #:seon.task{:name "changed-identities-walks-history-for-an-adopted-commit"
             :kind :function
             :intent "changed-identities answered 0 identities in 1.2 s on default (2026-09-28) when the cluster's adopted commit was the published commit: with no publication report it reads (d/since (d/history after) basis) over the whole history index, so its cost follows the store, not the change. Done when an equal prior and published commit answers #{} without reading history and a real diff costs what changed, with a timing regression."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/changed-identities}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/boot-reliability--candidate.sh"}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "index-issues-drops-every-opened-date-in-a-git-less-archive",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "seon.cluster.source/index-issues! (source.clj:523) indexes notes from an archive without .git, so every :seon.issue/opened is silently missing in production adoption. Done when the opened dates come from a declared source (the checkout's git) or the gap is recorded as a typed unknown, with a regression on an archive without .git. The issue namespaces are cut-planned (owner ruling 2026-09-28: cut only after tasks hold an ordered series); if the cut lands first, delete this task with it.",
            :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/changed-identities}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-new-function-cannot-arm-in-the-merge-gate"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Live on default (2026-10-08, spec-merge dogfood), every proposal adding a contracted function in a new namespace prepares red: the proof that was green on the agent's branch errors on the candidate with 'Cannot interpret scratch.merge-dogfood3/sq: this context holds no Var to arm its contract on.' (`seon.sci.eval/install-function-contract!`). Hypothesis, unverified: the candidate's install-row! takes the evaluated? branch (mark-installed! then arm) for a row this context never evaluated. host-test a-proposal-waits-for-its-assigned-tasks-proofs-then-reaches-root passes in its fixture world, so the live path differs. Done when a new function in a new namespace, proposed through MCP SCI, prepares green on a disposable target, as a regression through the real entrance."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! seon.cluster/candidate-gate! seon.sci.eval/install-row!}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-only-namespace-throws-at-prepare"
             :kind :function
             :intent "prepare-merge! of a proposal whose branch adds only a deftest in a new namespace throws 'Nothing found for entity id [`:seon.ns/name` scratch.merge-dogfood4]' (entity-id/missing) out of `my.program/prepare-merge!` in 77 ms, live 2026-10-08: the reconcile transaction names the namespace by lookup ref before the namespace row exists on the destination, or the namespace row is missing from the delta. A returning tool also must not throw. Done when that proposal prepares, regression through MCP SCI."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! seon.fn/reconcile-tx}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-branch-schema-blocks-diff-and-merge"
             :kind :function
             :intent "One `seon.schema/register!` on an agent branch makes `my.program/diff` and compare-branch refuse the whole comparison with 'Only function, test and namespace declarations may change here.' (`seon.program/changed-identities`), though digest-map compares `:seon.schema/key` as a family. A schema change can neither be seen nor merged. Done when diff lists the schema key as added/changed with its stored-data check against the target, and prepare carries it, regression through MCP SCI."
             :must-read #{seon.program/changed-identities seon.cluster.source/compare-branch my.program/diff}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-red-prepare-leaks-its-candidate-branch"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A prepare whose proofs are red leaves its candidate branch (:merge-<id>) in the store roster; only a successful accept retires it, so each refused review leaks one branch. Done when the candidate's lifetime is the merge task's: retired when the task closes (accepted or declined) or when a new prepare replaces it."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge! my.program/accept-merge!}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-launch-option-change-in-deps-edn-refuses-every-check"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/dependency-digests` hashes deps.edn's whole bytes, so classify-paths refuses RESTART NEEDED for a change to an alias's :jvm-opts alone, which loads no different dependency (2026-10-08, lane dials-16gb: removing -XX:MaxRAMPercentage from :dev/:test was refused by bin/seon check on default twice, once right after a fresh restart). No deps.edn change can then pass bin/lane commit, because default only ever runs committed bytes. The class cache already keys on deps.edn without launch options (`seon.dev.dependency-digest/dependency-declarations`). Done when a deps.edn change confined to launch options (:jvm-opts :main-opts :exec-fn :exec-args :ns-default :ns-aliases) publishes and a dependency change still refuses, as a regression."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/dependency-digests seon.cluster.source/classify-paths}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-dependency-addition-cannot-be-proven-with-its-first-consumer"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/classify-paths` refuses RESTART NEEDED for any changed deps.edn in a candidate, so bin/seon check cannot prove a slice that adds a dependency together with the code that needs it (2026-10-08, lane impl-ref1: REF-1 adds commonmark-ext-heading-anchor and commonmark-ext-yaml-front-matter for the new `seon.ref`; the check refused before booting its throwaway JVM). The throwaway JVM launches from the candidate archive's own classpath (`clojure -Spath` in the archive), so it could load the new jars; only the rehearsal on default cannot. The slice then waits for root to commit deps.edn alone and restart default before its proof runs. Done when a candidate adding a Maven dependency and a namespace importing its classes passes bin/seon check with that namespace's test, and default's publication still refuses until restart, as a regression."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/classify-paths}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-candidate-script-namespace-is-analyzed-as-the-running-jvms-copy"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon check rehearses a candidate on default, and seon.fn.analyzer lints the checkout's non-program roots (script/) from the running JVM's own source directory, not the candidate's. A candidate that adds a var to seon.operator and a test/ deftest calling it is refused 'Unresolved var: `seon.operator/launch-argv`' (2026-10-08, lane dials-16gb), so no script change can land with its JVM test in one slice. Done when the rehearsal analyzes the candidate's script/ files and that pair passes, as a regression."
             :must-read #{seon.fn.analyzer/ensure-dependency-analysis! seon.cluster/rehearse-source!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-second-write-back-conflicts-with-the-branchs-own-first"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Lane repl-only-2 (default 35e95e7b3, 2026-10-09) wrote its branch back with `my.program/write-branch!` (adding `my.program-test/an-agent-reads-and-writes-files-as-clojure-does`), redefined that test on the branch, and a second `write-branch!` refused :conflict: `:seon.program/conflict-digests` named base `:seon.program/absent`, head = the digest its own first write-back put in the file, branch = the new one. The write-back base does not advance past the branch's own write, so iterating on a written-back declaration needs an outside edit. Done when a second write-back of the same branch replaces what its first wrote, with a regression."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch! seon.cluster.source/write-back!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "merge-base-survives-collection"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/merge-base` walked parent commits, but `seon.cluster.registry/retention-cutoff` collection (Datahike `datahike.gc/reachable-in-branch`) keeps every branch head and follows a commit's parents only while that commit is newer than the cutoff, so a branch idle past `:seon.config.db/snapshot-window-ms` lost its fork commit: on default 2026-10-10 the walk refused on 31 of 37 agent and checkpoint branches with \"The merge base is unavailable within its commit bound.\", so `my.program/diff`, `prepare-merge!` and `my.program/write-branch!` (`seon.cluster.source/compare-branch`) refused. Done when the merge base is the branch's own value `d/as-of` its base-t toward its target: the branch's fork entity (`seon.cluster.registry/fork!`, `seon.cluster.registry/branch-fork-entity`) carries a fork id minted by `seon.id`, its target and its base-t attribute (the fork `t`, or for a fork of a work branch that branch's base toward the same target); each merge advances the base, `seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` writing the fork entity on the target with the merged value's `t`, matched by fork id so a reused branch name never takes a retired fork's base; the parent walk and its bound deleted; a missing fork entity and a comparison with a target the fork entity does not name refuse by name; `an-idle-branch-keeps-the-commit-it-forked-from-through-collection`, `a-reused-branch-name-never-takes-the-retired-forks-merged-base`, `seon.cluster.merge-test/a-fork-of-a-work-branch-compares-with-the-target-through-its-parents-changes` and `seon.cluster.merge-test/a-candidate-merges-only-its-tested-green-program` green."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/merge-base seon.cluster.source/compare-branch seon.cluster.source/accept-merge! seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.cluster.registry/branch-fork-entity seon.cluster.registry/fork-of seon.cluster.registry/retention-cutoff}}
 #:seon.task{:name "as-of-merge-base-digest-reads-are-slower"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.source/compare-branch` reads `seon.program/digest-map` over the merge base, now a `d/as-of` value (`seon.cluster.source/merge-base`). Measured on default 2026-10-10 (branch :agent-e91b525fbb9f, 5 changed identities): 35 ms over the as-of value against 5 ms over the materialized fork commit, 7x, because as-of filters the history index per read. Done when the base digest read costs what the commit read costs (for instance one `d/since`-bounded history read of the changed identities' digest datoms at the base-t), with a timing regression through `seon.profile/measure`."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/merge-base seon.cluster.source/compare-branch seon.program/digest-map}}
 #:seon.task{:name "write-back-of-a-new-require-analyses-the-published-snapshot"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A `require` added to a test namespace's ns form at the REPL is refused by `my.program/write-branch!` (lane loop-prove, 86b7cdbbe, 2026-10-10) as \"Unresolved namespace seon.db\": `seon.cluster.source/write-back!` proves the candidate by analysing it (`seon.cluster.source/proven-file`, `seon.fn.analyzer/invoke-kondo`) against the published HEAD snapshot (data/source/<sha>), whose analysis lacks the namespaces the branch's own new require makes reachable, so a require the branch is adding cannot resolve. Not verified at the REPL here (the repro writes a file); the sibling `seon.turn.batch-test/a-repl-require-is-written-back-in-the-ns-form-and-reindexes-equal` proves the plain case calling `write-back!` directly. Done when a branch that adds a require to an ns form and a form using it writes back and reindexes equal, asserted on the written paths."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/write-back! seon.cluster.source/proven-file my.program/write-branch! seon.fn.analyzer/invoke-kondo}}
 #:seon.task{:name "held-digests-refuses-a-map-on-the-write-back-path"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An open fault on default a6cbe4fcf (2026-10-10, runtime_status): `seon.cluster.source/held-digests` refused its result at `[[:seon.program/sym seon.sci.forgive/scan]]`, \"expected :seon.program/definition-digest (a string of length exactly 64), got a map\", contract `:seon.source/expected-digests`, called from `seon.cluster.source/write-back-commits`. A write-back whose accepted merge holds that identity fails before any file is read; the identity attribute `:seon.program/sym` is not one of `:seon.fn/sym`, `:seon.test/sym`, `:seon.ns/name`. Not reproduced by lane merge-path. Done when the producer of that accepted identity is found and `held-digests` answers a digest per identity, with a regression."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/held-digests seon.cluster.source/write-back-commits seon.cluster.source/write-back!}}

 #:seon.task{:name "accepting-a-one-function-merge-allocates-hundreds-of-megabytes"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` of a one-function, one-test proposal (lane merge-path, 2026-10-10, default a6cbe4fcf, gate run e5d251ed2dd7) allocated 733 MB in 322 ms when accepted and 2.0 GB when it refused (custody mismatch). What the work must do: check the touched entities' digests, transact the selected declarations onto the target and record the merge, proportional to the declarations merged, not to the program. Profile the accept with `seon.profile/measure`, name the armed functions that allocate, and fix the owner; done when a one-function accept allocates within 10x of its datoms' size. Chain run 5 (2026-10-10, default 340462543, the one-function proposal `my.program/match-row`, gate run 74e4621e2005) measured the whole merge, so the task covers prepare too: `my.program/prepare-merge!` 4.0 s and 1.1 GB; `my.program/accept-merge!` 1.0 s and 1.4 GB; root's reaching-test step after the accept 2.5 s and 2.2 GB. Each is over the one-second line (CLAUDE.md: name the armed functions that account for it, or fix it) and none is proportional to the declarations merged; profile all three, prepare first."
  :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/accept-merge! seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!}
  :evidence #{"docs/seon/plan.md"}}]
